<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fall of Singapore</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> was the surrender of the British garrison to Japanese forces in February 1942, after a rapid campaign through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>. It was one of the greatest triumphs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and a severe blow to British imperial prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore.jpg" alt="Fall of Singapore" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer not identified. &quot;Official photograph&quot;. Post-Work: W.wolny · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surrender_Singapore.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> was the foremost British military base and economic port in South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>. After the Anglo-Japanese alliance lapsed in 1923 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> built a naval base there, a key part of its interwar defence planning for the region, though financial constraints hampered construction. In November 1940 the German raider Atlantis captured the British steamer Automedon, whose papers described the weakness of the Singapore base, and the Germans passed copies to the Japanese. The Japanese had also broken the British Army's codes.</p><p>The Japanese 25th Army invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> on 8 December 1941, almost simultaneously with the attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a>. Conventional British thinking held that the Japanese were inferior and the jungle impassable, and the Japanese repeatedly outflanked hastily established defensive lines. Their general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomoyuki-yamashita">Tomoyuki Yamashita</a> advanced with about 30,000 men down the Malayan peninsula.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Lieutenant-General Arthur Percival commanded 85,000 Allied troops at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, many of them under-strength and inexperienced. The British destroyed the causeway across the Johor Strait, which delayed the attack for over a week. After a bombardment of 88,000 shells, the first Japanese wave crossed shortly before 20:30 on 8 February 1942. The main weight, about 13,000 men, fell on the 22nd Australian Brigade, which had about 3,000 men and no significant reserve. Percival had expected a crossing in the north and did not reinforce the brigade until the morning of 9 February.</p><p>On 14 February the Japanese broke through towards the Alexandra Barracks Hospital, where they killed soldiers, doctors and nurses. By 15 February about a million civilians were crammed into 1 per cent of the island, the water system was badly damaged and ammunition was nearly gone. After a conference at Fort Canning at 09:30 all agreed that no counter-attack was possible. For the second time Yamashita demanded unconditional surrender, and on the afternoon of 15 February Percival capitulated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>About 80,000 British, Indian, Australian and local troops became prisoners of war, joining the 50,000 taken in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, and many died of neglect, abuse or forced labour. About 40,000 Indian soldiers, mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscripts</a>, joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-army">Indian National Army</a> and fought with the Japanese in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> campaign. The Japanese renamed the city Syonan-to and murdered thousands of civilians, mostly ethnic Chinese, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sook-ching">Sook Ching massacre</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> called it the worst disaster and largest capitulation in British history. Together with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> and the sinking of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> and Repulse two months earlier, it severely undermined British prestige in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, which contributed to the end of British colonial rule in the region after the war. The Australian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-curtin">John Curtin</a> said the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> opened the Battle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. Singapore has marked Total Defence Day on 15 February since 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fall of Singapore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Japan's rapid conquest of Hong Kong, Malaya, Singapore and Burma in 1941–1942, the <b>fall of Singapore</b> in February 1942 above all, destroyed the myth of British invincibility in Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>fall of Singapore</b> in 1942 broke faith in British protection, and Australia adopted the Statute of Westminster in the same year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>By mid-1942, following the <b>fall of Singapore</b> in February, the conquest of the Dutch East Indies, and the extension of the Pacific perimeter to the Aleutian Islands, Japan controlled approximately seven million square kilometres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Military</a></li></ul></section>
